## TypeScript & Type Safety

- Strict mode is required. Never use `any` — use `unknown` when necessary.
- Domain/contract types come from `@lemonade/api-types` (generated). No hand-written response interfaces.
- Prefer interfaces, type aliases, discriminated unions.

---

## Error Handling

One normalized shape from the transport — nothing above it inspects raw axios errors:

```ts
type ApiError = {
  status: number;
  errorCode: ErrorCode;        // backend's 18-case enum
  message: string;
  fieldErrors?: Record<string, string[]>;
  correlationId?: string;
  kind: 'validation' | 'auth' | 'permission' | 'notFound'
      | 'conflict' | 'rateLimit' | 'server' | 'network' | 'timeout';
};
```

Every route group gets `error.tsx`, `not-found.tsx`, `loading.tsx`; plus a root `global-error.tsx`. Blast radius of a failure is one segment, not the app.

Timeouts on every request. Disable-on-submit everywhere. Idempotency keys on every money mutation. Never retry non-idempotent mutations automatically.

